/ActualText (Within the assigned zone, the leaf router directs data to specific leaf nodes. These leaf nodes manage target ranges, with data sharded lexicographically according to target strings. )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
161 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 299 0 R
/ActualText (Each leaf router keeps an updated range map that indicates which leaf node is responsible for each target range. This map is periodically refreshed by updates from leaf nodes, ensuring uninterrupted data collection even if the range assigner experiences temporary issues. )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
162 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Before going to the part where we will see how the query execution takes place, we will see some implementation details which actually solved those problems which were stated earlier)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
163 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Replication:)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
164 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 300 0 R
/ActualText (Replication Policy: Monarch allows users to select the number of replicas per target \(ranging from 1 to 3\), enabling a trade-off between availability and storage cost.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
165 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 301 0 R
/ActualText (Granularity Control: Users can choose to retain different levels of data detail for each replica, such as retaining only a fine-grained view on one replica and a coarser view on others.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
166 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Load Balancing:)
/E (Load Balancing:)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
167 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 302 0 R
/ActualText (The range assigner actively balances the load by moving, splitting, or merging ranges as needed based on CPU load and memory usage across leaf nodes)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
168 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Data Availability: )
/E (Data Availability: )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
169 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 303 0 R
/ActualText (During the transfer process, both the source and destination leaves temporarily collect and log data for range R to avoid any data loss and ensure continuous availability.\(Interesting optimisation\))
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
170 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Direct Updates from Leaves: Leaves keep leaf routers informed about range assignments, rather than relying on the range assigner for updates\(Interesting optimisation\) which makes system run smoothly despite failure of range assigner)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
171 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Scalability and Performance: )
/E (Scalability and Performance: )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
172 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 304 0 R
/ActualText <>
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
173 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 305 0 R
/ActualText (For high scalability, Monarch uses a field hints index \(FHI\), stored in index servers, to limit the load when sending a query from parent to children by skipping irrelevant children \(those without input data to the particular query\). This is implemented using n-grams for the data and storing what all leaves contain that.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
174 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Storage: For the vast incoming data which is at rate of petabytes per unit time, Monarch used delta-time encoding)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
175 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Monarch prioritizes high availability and partition tolerance over consistency. Interactions with strongly consistent databases, such as Spanner, can result in lengthy blocks, which are not suitable for Monarch. This blocking could prolong the meantimeto-detection and mean-time-to-mitigation during potential outages. To ensure timely alert delivery, Monarch focuses on providing the most recent data quickly)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
176 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Query Execution)
/E (Query Execution)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
177 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (In Monarch, there are two types of queries: )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
178 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 306 0 R
/ActualText (Ad hoc Queries These are one-time queries from users outside the system.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
179 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 307 0 R
/ActualText (Standing Queries These are periodic queries whose results are stored back in Monarch. Standing queries are used to generate alerts or pre-process data for faster and more cost-effective access later. They can be processed at either the regional zone level or the global root level, based on the type of data and query requirements. Most standing queries are handled at the zone level, making them more efficient and resilient to network issues.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
180 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Queries are processed in a three-level hierarchy:)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
181 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 308 0 R
/ActualText (Root Mixer: Receives the query and distributes it to zone mixers.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
182 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 309 0 R
/ActualText (Zone Mixers: Send the query to relevant leaf nodes based on an index. )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
183 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 310 0 R
/ActualText (Leaf Nodes: Process the data closest to the source.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
184 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText <>
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
185 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 311 0 R
/ActualText (Reducing the amount of data transferred to higher levels.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
186 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 312 0 R
/ActualText (Allowing more concurrent processing.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
187 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (For example, if a query only involves data from one zone, it can be fully processed there without needing root-level involvement. This pushdown approach makes 95% of standing queries complete within the zone, which also prevents cross-region data traffic and reduces latency.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
188 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Result Aggregation:)
/E (Result Aggregation:)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
189 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 313 0 R
/ActualText <>
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
190 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 314 0 R
/ActualText (Zone Mixers gather results from multiple leaf nodes in their region. They combine and further aggregate data, then send only summarized information to the root mixer.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
191 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 315 0 R
/ActualText (Root Mixer collects data from zone mixers. It completes any remaining aggregation, checks security, and applies final optimizations.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
192 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (My Observations)
/E (My Observations)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
193 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (While Monarch prioritizes high availability and partition tolerance, the trade-off with data consistency could lead to stale reads. This seemed more bold decision to me, instead we should be using better consistency model which does different things based on situation. )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
194 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
